  "notes": "West leads the ♦K against 6♠. West's diamond opening lead is won by South's ♦A. On the assumption that West was dealt the ♦Q (for his lead of the ♦K) the contract is assured even if West was dealt 4 hearts to the ♥J.\n\nSouth should draw trump and test hearts by cashing the ♥A and ♥Q. The contract will be easy unless East discards on this trick. In this case South should continue by running the rest of the spades, discarding clubs (or 1 heart and 1 club) from the dummy.\n\nWhen South plays a club to dummy's ♣A, West is strip squeezed into coming down to 2 hearts and the singleton ♦Q. South continues by leading dummy's remaining diamond and discarding his club loser as West wins the ♦Q. West is forced to lead away from his ♥J at trick 12, allowing South to win the last 2 tricks.\n\nThis basic strip squeeze position matures with 2 (or more) losers. A defender is squeezed into discarding either a winner or a safe exit card so that he can later be forced to lead into a tenace. Often the strip squeeze can come to declarer's rescue when he is unable to safely rectify the count down to the 1 loser that a simple squeeze requires. In this deal, if South had tried to lose an early trick, the defense would have returned a diamond to kill dummy's threat in that suit.",
  "commentary_paragraphs": [
    "West leads the ♦K against 6♠. West's diamond opening lead is won by South's ♦A. On the assumption that West was dealt the ♦Q (for his lead of the ♦K) the contract is assured even if West was dealt 4 hearts to the ♥J.",
    "South should draw trump and test hearts by cashing the ♥A and ♥Q. The contract will be easy unless East discards on this trick. In this case South should continue by running the rest of the spades, discarding clubs (or 1 heart and 1 club) from the dummy.",
    "When South plays a club to dummy's ♣A, West is strip squeezed into coming down to 2 hearts and the singleton ♦Q. South continues by leading dummy's remaining diamond and discarding his club loser as West wins the ♦Q. West is forced to lead away from his ♥J at trick 12, allowing South to win the last 2 tricks.",
    "This basic strip squeeze position matures with 2 (or more) losers. A defender is squeezed into discarding either a winner or a safe exit card so that he can later be forced to lead into a tenace. Often the strip squeeze can come to declarer's rescue when he is unable to safely rectify the count down to the 1 loser that a simple squeeze requires. In this deal, if South had tried to lose an early trick, the defense would have returned a diamond to kill dummy's threat in that suit."
  ],
